  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA mean upper low will continue to spin over northern Quebec near\nthe Labrador border. Periodic ridging and troughing will rotate\naround this low leading to hit or miss showers through midweek.\nAny showers this afternoon will diminish tonight into Tuesday \nas a progressive ridge axis crosses the forecast area. The next \nshortwave then arrives late Tuesday into Wednesday morning which\nwill bring more chances of showers. The higher peaks will \nlikely see some snow mixing in. More rain and snow showers will \nbe possible over the mountains on Wednesday but broad ridging \nwill be on approach so coverage may be more limited, especially \nlater in the day. \n\nTemperatures will generally be on a gradual cooling trend\nthrough the week, trending closer to normal for this time of\nyear. Tuesday's highs are expected to range from the low/mid \n40s north to the low/mid 50s south and then by Thursday most \nwill see highs in the 40s (lower 50s confined to southern NH and\ncoastal ME). By Friday, highs will only range from the mid 30s \nto mid 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDeterministic global models have further diverged regarding the\ntrack of a late week system with the 09.12Z GFS continuing to \nfavor a track swinging inland and the Euro now whiffing on its \nlast four runs (08.18Z to 09.12Z). The 09.12Z Canadian has also \ninterestingly taken a large jump northward and is more in line \nwith the GFS. Ensembles have remained more consistent with GEFS \nshowing the low centers clustered around Cape Cod and the Gulf \nof Maine, but the clustering in the ECMWF has taken a notable \nshift eastward out to sea. This does increase confidence in the \nfact that this system will ultimately take a coastal track so \nwith that in mind lets break down the potential impacts in a \nprobabilistic sense since the envelope of track solutions is a \nlarge range. \n  \nSNOW: The latest NBM probabilities of measurable snow are low \nwith the higher peaks of the white mountains boasting anything \nover 50%. Outside of the mountains down through the foothills \nprobabilities are in the 40% range. This leaves the coastal \nplain at less than 30% and near 0% at the immediate coast. So at\nthis point I have confidence in some degree of high elevation \nsnow with less impacts outside of the mountains, but amounts \neither way are too track dependent to try and pin down this \nearly.\n\nPTYPE: It is either going to be rain or snow and with current \ntrends and aforementioned snow probabilities any snow looks like\nit would be confined to the mountains, with maybe more wet \nsnowflakes down through the foothills. Lastly, it seems like any\nprecipitation down at the coast would be mainly a cold rain. \n\nWIND: Confidence is moderate that the highest winds are going \nto be at the coast, but magnitude is much more uncertain due to \nhow much track factors into this. Yesterday long range ensembles\nhad widespread 25 to 30 mph winds, but now with the ECMWF's \nwestward trends global blends are hard pressed to give you 25 \nmph. Overall, I think it is fair to say unless there is a \ndrastic shift in guidance wind seems low impact.\n\nIn the wake of this system mean troughing persists over the \nNortheast, but the trend is going to be toward a return to more \nseasonable temperatures and drier weather heading into next \nweek.",
